{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XnCLcnwyxK85MWqxkvBm8m","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tid: srd106-df\n2\ttitle: \"cmd/df: Report Filesystem Disk Space Usage\"\n3\t\n4\tproblem: |\n5\t  df reports filesystem disk space usage, but macOS BSD df and GNU df differ in default\n6\t  block size (512-byte vs 1024-byte blocks), column headers, output alignment, and flag\n7\t  availability (--output absent in BSD df, -T absent in BSD df). Scripts that parse df\n8\t  output break when moving between platforms. The Go implementation must match the Homebrew\n9\t  GNU reference binary (gdf, installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all in-scope flag\n10\t  combinations, verified by differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n11\t\n12\t  df requires pkg/sys for filesystem metadata (statvfs for block counts, device resolution)\n13\t  and for SIGPIPE handling. These shared components are specified in srd002-sys.\n14\t\n15\tgoals:\n16\t  - G1: Report filesystem disk space usage for mounted filesystems, showing total, used, available, use percentage, and mount point.\n17\t  - G2: Support human-readable output (-h for binary, -H for SI units).\n18\t  - G3: Support filesystem type display (-T) and type-based filtering (-t, -x).\n19\t  - G4: Support inode reporting (-i) and customizable column selection (--output).\n20\t\n21\trequirements:\n22\t  R1:\n23\t    title: Default output formatting\n24\t    items:\n25\t      - R1.1:\n26\t          text: |\n27\t            When invoked with no arguments, must list all mounted filesystems excluding\n28\t            pseudo-filesystems (those with 0 total blocks), displaying one line per filesystem.\n29\t            Default columns are: Filesystem, 1K-blocks, Used, Available, Use%, Mounted on.\n30\t            Sizes are reported in 1024-byte block units.\n31\t      - R1.2:\n32\t          text: |\n33\t            Must print a header line as the first line of output. Column headers must match\n34\t            GNU df exactly: \"Filesystem\", \"1K-blocks\", \"Used\", \"Available\", \"Use%\",\n35\t            \"Mounted on\". Numeric columns (1K-blocks, Used, Available, Use%) are right-aligned.\n36\t            Filesystem and Mounted on are left-aligned.\n37\t      - R1.3:\n38\t          text: |\n39\t            Use% is calculated as ceiling((total - available) * 100 / total) when total > 0.\n40\t            When total is 0, Use% is displayed as a dash \"-\". The percentage is followed by\n41\t            \"%\" with no space. Values are right-aligned in a column wide enough for \"100%\".\n42\t      - R1.4:\n43\t          text: |\n44\t            When one or more FILE arguments are given, must report the filesystem containing\n45\t            each FILE rather than all filesystems. If a FILE does not exist, must print a\n46\t            diagnostic to stderr and continue processing remaining arguments.\n47\t      - R1.5:\n48\t          text: |\n49\t            Column widths must be computed as per-column maxima of entry lengths across all\n50\t            rows including the header, matching GNU df alignment. Fields are separated by\n51\t            at least one space.\n52\t\n53\t  R2:\n54\t    title: Human-readable and SI output\n55\t    items:\n56\t      - R2.1:\n57\t          text: |\n58\t            -h or --human-readable must display sizes using binary units (1024-based: K, M, G,\n59\t            T, P, E) via pkg/format.HumanSize(bytes int64, opts HumanSizeOpts) string with\n60\t            opts.Binary = true. The column header changes from \"1K-blocks\" to \"Size\". -h\n61\t            applies to the Size, Used, and Available columns.\n62\t      - R2.2:\n63\t          text: |\n64\t            -H or --si must display sizes using SI units (1000-based: kB, MB, GB, TB, PB, EB)\n65\t            via pkg/format.HumanSize(bytes int64, opts HumanSizeOpts) string with opts.Binary =\n66\t            false. The column header changes from \"1K-blocks\" to \"Size\". -H applies to the\n67\t            Size, Used, and Available columns.\n68\t      - R2.3:\n69\t          text: -h and -H are mutually exclusive. The last one on the command line takes precedence.\n70\t\n71\t  R3:\n72\t    title: Filesystem type, inode display, and column selection\n73\t    items:\n74\t      - R3.1:\n75\t          text: |\n76\t            -T or --print-type must insert a \"Type\" column after the Filesystem column,\n77\t            showing the filesystem type (e.g., apfs, ext4, tmpfs) for each entry.\n78\t      - R3.2:\n79\t          text: |\n80\t            -i or --inodes must display inode information instead of block usage. Columns\n81\t            become: Filesystem, Inodes, IUsed, IFree, IUse%, Mounted on. IUse% is calculated\n82\t            the same way as Use% but using inode counts instead of block counts.\n83\t      - R3.3:\n84\t          text: |\n85\t            -a or --all must include pseudo-filesystems (those with 0 total blocks) in the\n86\t            output. Without -a, filesystems with 0 total blocks are excluded.\n87\t      - R3.4:\n88\t          text: |\n89\t            -l or --local must restrict output to local filesystems only, excluding network\n90\t            filesystems (NFS, SMB, CIFS, etc.).\n91\t      - R3.5:\n92\t          text: |\n93\t            -t TYPE or --type=TYPE must include only filesystems of the specified type.\n94\t            Multiple -t flags may be given to include multiple types. When -t is given,\n95\t            only filesystems matching at least one specified type are shown.\n96\t      - R3.6:\n97\t          text: |\n98\t            -x TYPE or --exclude-type=TYPE must exclude filesystems of the specified type.\n99\t            Multiple -x flags may be given. When both -t and -x are given, -t inclusion\n100\t            is applied first, then -x exclusion.\n101\t      - R3.7:\n102\t          text: |\n103\t            --output or --output=FIELD_LIST must select which columns to display. When\n104\t            --output is given without a field list, all available fields are shown. When\n105\t            a comma-separated field list is given, only those fields appear in the specified\n106\t            order. Available fields: source, fstype, itotal, iused, iavail, ipcent, size,\n107\t            used, avail, pcent, file, target. --output is incompatible with -i, -T, and -h;\n108\t            if combined, must print an error and exit 1.\n109\t\n110\t  R4:\n111\t    title: Exit codes and signal handling\n112\t    items:\n113\t      - R4.1:\n114\t          text: Must exit 0 when all filesystems are reported successfully.\n115\t      - R4.2:\n116\t          text: Must exit 1 when any error occurs (inaccessible FILE argument, invalid option). Must print a diagnostic to stderr for each error.\n117\t      - R4.3:\n118\t          text: Must install a SIGPIPE handler at startup by calling pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() so that piping df output to head or other truncating consumers does not produce a broken-pipe error message.\n119\t\n120\tnon_goals:\n121\t  - --sync (invoke sync before getting usage info) is out of scope.\n122\t  - --no-sync is accepted but has no effect (it is the default).\n123\t  - --total (produce a grand total line) is out of scope.\n124\t  - -P (POSIX output format) is out of scope; default output matches GNU df.\n125\t  - --block-size=SIZE with arbitrary unit suffixes is out of scope; only -h, -H, and default 1K blocks are supported.\n126\t  - -k is accepted but has no visible effect (1K is the default).\n127\t  - Reporting on individual block devices or disk partitions beyond what the OS mounts is out of scope.\n128\t\n129\tacceptance_criteria:\n130\t  - id: AC1\n131\t    criterion: \"Default mode comparison -- gdf and go run ./cmd/df produce identical output for mounted filesystems when run on the same machine. Differential tests must compare column headers, alignment, and Use% values.\"\n132\t    traces:\n133\t      - R1.1\n134\t      - R1.2\n135\t      - R1.3\n136\t      - R1.5\n137\t  - id: AC2\n138\t    criterion: \"Human-readable sizes -- gdf -h and go run ./cmd/df -h produce identical size strings for all columns.\"\n139\t    traces:\n140\t      - R2.1\n141\t      - R2.3\n142\t  - id: AC3\n143\t    criterion: \"SI units -- gdf -H and go run ./cmd/df -H produce identical size strings for all columns.\"\n144\t    traces:\n145\t      - R2.2\n146\t      - R2.3\n147\t  - id: AC4\n148\t    criterion: \"Filesystem type display -- gdf -T and go run ./cmd/df -T produce identical output including the Type column.\"\n149\t    traces:\n150\t      - R3.1\n151\t  - id: AC5\n152\t    criterion: \"Inode display -- gdf -i and go run ./cmd/df -i produce identical inode columns and IUse% values.\"\n153\t    traces:\n154\t      - R3.2\n155\t  - id: AC6\n156\t    criterion: \"Type filtering -- gdf -t apfs and go run ./cmd/df -t apfs produce identical filtered output. gdf -x devfs and the Go binary with -x devfs exclude the same filesystems.\"\n157\t    traces:\n158\t      - R3.5\n159\t      - R3.6\n160\t  - id: AC7\n161\t    criterion: \"Output column selection -- gdf --output=source,size,used,avail,pcent,target and the Go binary produce identical column-selected output.\"\n162\t    traces:\n163\t      - R3.7\n164\t  - id: AC8\n165\t    criterion: \"FILE argument -- gdf / and go run ./cmd/df / produce identical output for the root filesystem.\"\n166\t    traces:\n167\t      - R1.4\n168\t  - id: AC9\n169\t    criterion: \"Exit codes -- the Go binary exits 1 when given a non-existent file argument and prints a diagnostic to stderr, matching gdf behavior.\"\n170\t    traces:\n171\t      - R4.1\n172\t      - R4.2\n173\t      - R4.3\n174\t  - id: AC10\n175\t    criterion: \"cmd/df compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n176\t    traces:\n177\t      - R1.1\n178\t      - R1.2\n179\t      - R1.3\n180\t      - R1.4\n181\t      - R1.5\n182\t      - R2.1\n183\t      - R2.2\n184\t      - R2.3\n185\t      - R3.1\n186\t      - R3.2\n187\t      - R3.3\n188\t      - R3.4\n189\t      - R3.5\n190\t      - R3.6\n191\t      - R3.7\n192\t      - R4.1\n193\t      - R4.2\n194\t      - R4.3\n195\t\n196\tpackage_contract:\n197\t  imports:\n198\t    - github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils/pkg/format\n199\t    - github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils/pkg/sys\n200\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e6b56990-b444-4142-be23-d97fbc698916","uuid":"29e6c21f-9741-4613-a002-791ac29a436d","timestamp":"2026-05-27T19:02:46.426Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5605/docs/specs/software-requirements/srd106-df.yaml","content":"id: srd106-df\ntitle: \"cmd/df: Report Filesystem Disk Space Usage\"\n\nproblem: |\n  df reports filesystem disk space usage, but macOS BSD df and GNU df differ in default\n  block size (512-byte vs 1024-byte blocks), column headers, output alignment, and flag\n  availability (--output absent in BSD df, -T absent in BSD df). Scripts that parse df\n  output break when moving between platforms. The Go implementation must match the Homebrew\n  GNU reference binary (gdf, installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all in-scope flag\n  combinations, verified by differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n\n  df requires pkg/sys for filesystem metadata (statvfs for block counts, device resolution)\n  and for SIGPIPE handling. These shared components are specified in srd002-sys.\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Report filesystem disk space usage for mounted filesystems, showing total, used, available, use percentage, and mount point.\n  - G2: Support human-readable output (-h for binary, -H for SI units).\n  - G3: Support filesystem type display (-T) and type-based filtering (-t, -x).\n  - G4: Support inode reporting (-i) and customizable column selection (--output).\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Default output formatting\n    items:\n      - R1.1:\n          text: |\n            When invoked with no arguments, must list all mounted filesystems excluding\n            pseudo-filesystems (those with 0 total blocks), displaying one line per filesystem.\n            Default columns are: Filesystem, 1K-blocks, Used, Available, Use%, Mounted on.\n            Sizes are reported in 1024-byte block units.\n      - R1.2:\n          text: |\n            Must print a header line as the first line of output. Column headers must match\n            GNU df exactly: \"Filesystem\", \"1K-blocks\", \"Used\", \"Available\", \"Use%\",\n            \"Mounted on\". Numeric columns (1K-blocks, Used, Available, Use%) are right-aligned.\n            Filesystem and Mounted on are left-aligned.\n      - R1.3:\n          text: |\n            Use% is calculated as ceiling((total - available) * 100 / total) when total > 0.\n            When total is 0, Use% is displayed as a dash \"-\". The percentage is followed by\n            \"%\" with no space. Values are right-aligned in a column wide enough for \"100%\".\n      - R1.4:\n          text: |\n            When one or more FILE arguments are given, must report the filesystem containing\n            each FILE rather than all filesystems. If a FILE does not exist, must print a\n            diagnostic to stderr and continue processing remaining arguments.\n      - R1.5:\n          text: |\n            Column widths must be computed as per-column maxima of entry lengths across all\n            rows including the header, matching GNU df alignment. Fields are separated by\n            at least one space.\n\n  R2:\n    title: Human-readable and SI output\n    items:\n      - R2.1:\n          text: |\n            -h or --human-readable must display sizes using binary units (1024-based: K, M, G,\n            T, P, E) via pkg/format.HumanSize(bytes int64, opts HumanSizeOpts) string with\n            opts.Binary = true. The column header changes from \"1K-blocks\" to \"Size\". -h\n            applies to the Size, Used, and Available columns.\n      - R2.2:\n          text: |\n            -H or --si must display sizes using SI units (1000-based: kB, MB, GB, TB, PB, EB)\n            via pkg/format.HumanSize(bytes int64, opts HumanSizeOpts) string with opts.Binary =\n            false. The column header changes from \"1K-blocks\" to \"Size\". -H applies to the\n            Size, Used, and Available columns.\n      - R2.3:\n          text: -h and -H are mutually exclusive. The last one on the command line takes precedence.\n\n  R3:\n    title: Filesystem type, inode display, and column selection\n    items:\n      - R3.1:\n          text: |\n            -T or --print-type must insert a \"Type\" column after the Filesystem column,\n            showing the filesystem type (e.g., apfs, ext4, tmpfs) for each entry.\n      - R3.2:\n          text: |\n            -i or --inodes must display inode information instead of block usage. Columns\n            become: Filesystem, Inodes, IUsed, IFree, IUse%, Mounted on. IUse% is calculated\n            the same way as Use% but using inode counts instead of block counts.\n      - R3.3:\n          text: |\n            -a or --all must include pseudo-filesystems (those with 0 total blocks) in the\n            output. Without -a, filesystems with 0 total blocks are excluded.\n      - R3.4:\n          text: |\n            -l or --local must restrict output to local filesystems only, excluding network\n            filesystems (NFS, SMB, CIFS, etc.).\n      - R3.5:\n          text: |\n            -t TYPE or --type=TYPE must include only filesystems of the specified type.\n            Multiple -t flags may be given to include multiple types. When -t is given,\n            only filesystems matching at least one specified type are shown.\n      - R3.6:\n          text: |\n            -x TYPE or --exclude-type=TYPE must exclude filesystems of the specified type.\n            Multiple -x flags may be given. When both -t and -x are given, -t inclusion\n            is applied first, then -x exclusion.\n      - R3.7:\n          text: |\n            --output or --output=FIELD_LIST must select which columns to display. When\n            --output is given without a field list, all available fields are shown. When\n            a comma-separated field list is given, only those fields appear in the specified\n            order. Available fields: source, fstype, itotal, iused, iavail, ipcent, size,\n            used, avail, pcent, file, target. --output is incompatible with -i, -T, and -h;\n            if combined, must print an error and exit 1.\n\n  R4:\n    title: Exit codes and signal handling\n    items:\n      - R4.1:\n          text: Must exit 0 when all filesystems are reported successfully.\n      - R4.2:\n          text: Must exit 1 when any error occurs (inaccessible FILE argument, invalid option). Must print a diagnostic to stderr for each error.\n      - R4.3:\n          text: Must install a SIGPIPE handler at startup by calling pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() so that piping df output to head or other truncating consumers does not produce a broken-pipe error message.\n\nnon_goals:\n  - --sync (invoke sync before getting usage info) is out of scope.\n  - --no-sync is accepted but has no effect (it is the default).\n  - --total (produce a grand total line) is out of scope.\n  - -P (POSIX output format) is out of scope; default output matches GNU df.\n  - --block-size=SIZE with arbitrary unit suffixes is out of scope; only -h, -H, and default 1K blocks are supported.\n  - -k is accepted but has no visible effect (1K is the default).\n  - Reporting on individual block devices or disk partitions beyond what the OS mounts is out of scope.\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - id: AC1\n    criterion: \"Default mode comparison -- gdf and go run ./cmd/df produce identical output for mounted filesystems when run on the same machine. Differential tests must compare column headers, alignment, and Use% values.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.2\n      - R1.3\n      - R1.5\n  - id: AC2\n    criterion: \"Human-readable sizes -- gdf -h and go run ./cmd/df -h produce identical size strings for all columns.\"\n    traces:\n      - R2.1\n      - R2.3\n  - id: AC3\n    criterion: \"SI units -- gdf -H and go run ./cmd/df -H produce identical size strings for all columns.\"\n    traces:\n      - R2.2\n      - R2.3\n  - id: AC4\n    criterion: \"Filesystem type display -- gdf -T and go run ./cmd/df -T produce identical output including the Type column.\"\n    traces:\n      - R3.1\n  - id: AC5\n    criterion: \"Inode display -- gdf -i and go run ./cmd/df -i produce identical inode columns and IUse% values.\"\n    traces:\n      - R3.2\n  - id: AC6\n    criterion: \"Type filtering -- gdf -t apfs and go run ./cmd/df -t apfs produce identical filtered output. gdf -x devfs and the Go binary with -x devfs exclude the same filesystems.\"\n    traces:\n      - R3.5\n      - R3.6\n  - id: AC7\n    criterion: \"Output column selection -- gdf --output=source,size,used,avail,pcent,target and the Go binary produce identical column-selected output.\"\n    traces:\n      - R3.7\n  - id: AC8\n    criterion: \"FILE argument -- gdf / and go run ./cmd/df / produce identical output for the root filesystem.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.4\n  - id: AC9\n    criterion: \"Exit codes -- the Go binary exits 1 when given a non-existent file argument and prints a diagnostic to stderr, matching gdf behavior.\"\n    traces:\n      - R4.1\n      - R4.2\n      - R4.3\n  - id: AC10\n    criterion: \"cmd/df compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.2\n      - R1.3\n      - R1.4\n      - R1.5\n      - R2.1\n      - R2.2\n      - R2.3\n      - R3.1\n      - R3.2\n      - R3.3\n      - R3.4\n      - R3.5\n      - R3.6\n      - R3.7\n      - R4.1\n      - R4.2\n      - R4.3\n\npackage_contract:\n  imports:\n    - github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils/pkg/format\n    - github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils/pkg/sys\n","numLines":200,"startLine":1,"tota